        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12364833/comment/12485209",
          "id": "12485209",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=angela",
            "name": "angela",
            "key": "anchela",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?avatarId=10452",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&avatarId=10452",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&avatarId=10452",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&avatarId=10452"
            },
            "displayName": "Angela Schreiber",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Etc/UTC"
          },
          "body": "i just happen to read that jsr170 makes an explicit statement regarding this:\n\n\"However, as opposed to the semantics of XPath, a name in a content repository path that does not explicitly specify an index implies an index of 1. For example, /a/b/c is equivalent to /a[1]/b[1]/c[1].\"\n\n",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=angela",
            "name": "angela",
            "key": "anchela",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?avatarId=10452",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&avatarId=10452",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&avatarId=10452",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&avatarId=10452"
            },
            "displayName": "Angela Schreiber",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Etc/UTC"
          },
          "created": "2007-03-29T15:14:23.382+0000",
          "updated": "2007-03-29T15:14:23.382+0000"
        },
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12364833/comment/12485340",
          "id": "12485340",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=jukkaz",
            "name": "jukkaz",
            "key": "jukkaz",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130"
            },
            "displayName": "Jukka Zitting",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "America/New_York"
          },
          "body": "Sounds like a correct change to me.\n\nBTW, is there even a need for the INDEX_UNDEFINED case? In other words, could we always normalize path components to internally always have an index that is >= 1? If the index is 1, then the \"[n]\" part would be skipped during string serialization.\n\nAnother note about this issue, does the comparison failure cause a regression against documented behaviour? If not, then I'd categorize this as an improvement rather than a bug.",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=jukkaz",
            "name": "jukkaz",
            "key": "jukkaz",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=jukkaz&avatarId=15130"
            },
            "displayName": "Jukka Zitting",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "America/New_York"
          },
          "created": "2007-03-29T20:32:55.614+0000",
          "updated": "2007-03-29T20:32:55.614+0000"
        },
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12364833/comment/12485342",
          "id": "12485342",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=reschke",
            "name": "reschke",
            "key": "reschke",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677"
            },
            "displayName": "Julian Reschke",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Europe/Berlin"
          },
          "body": "> BTW, is there even a need for the INDEX_UNDEFINED case? In other words, could we always normalize path components to internally always have an index that is >= 1? If the index is 1, then the \"[n]\" part would be skipped during string serialization. \n\nUnless it's used somewhere where relative paths are matched (such as in Node.getnNodes(relpath)).\n\n> Another note about this issue, does the comparison failure cause a regression against documented behaviour? If not, then I'd categorize this as an improvement rather than a bug.\n\nI think it *did* cause a failure for me in JCR2SPI, until I adjusted my SPI implementation so that Path objects were built exactly the way expected by JCR2SPI (because that one is using Path.equals to compare paths).\n\nTo summarize: we either should change .equals(), or, if we can't, provide an additional, weaker comparison method.",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=reschke",
            "name": "reschke",
            "key": "reschke",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=reschke&avatarId=52677"
            },
            "displayName": "Julian Reschke",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Europe/Berlin"
          },
          "created": "2007-03-29T20:39:08.551+0000",
          "updated": "2007-03-29T20:39:08.551+0000"
        },
        {
          "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/issue/12364833/comment/12485435",
          "id": "12485435",
          "author": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=tripod",
            "name": "tripod",
            "key": "tripod",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=tripod&avatarId=17636",
              "24x24": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=small&ownerId=tripod&avatarId=17636",
              "16x16": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=xsmall&ownerId=tripod&avatarId=17636",
              "32x32": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?size=medium&ownerId=tripod&avatarId=17636"
            },
            "displayName": "Tobias Bocanegra",
            "active": true,
            "timeZone": "Asia/Tokyo"
          },
          "body": "i'm in favor of normalizing the index and also make it 0-based throughout the code. there are several places where confusion exists about if the index is 0- or 1-based, with statements like this: if (index == 0) { index = 1}\n\nsuggest to:\n- use a 0-based index (when storing as int) throughout the code (also in NodeState)\n- drop INDEX_UNDEFINED in PathElement\n- during serialization, index == 0 will not be translated into ...[1].\n\n",
          "updateAuthor": {
            "self": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/rest/api/2/user?username=tripod",
            "name": "tripod",
            "key": "tripod",
            "avatarUrls": {
              "48x48": "https://issues.apache.org/jira/secure/useravatar?ownerId=tripod&avatarId=17636",
